<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zweibrücken</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Deux-Ponts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zweibrücken is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>. It was the seat of the Counts of Zweibrücken and from 1453 of the Palatinate-Zweibrücken line of the Wittelsbachs, whose dukes later became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied it in 1793 and annexed it in 1797; it passed to Bavaria in 1816.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zweibrucken.jpg" alt="Zweibrücken" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas W. Jefferson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zweibr%C3%BCcken_castle_front_April_2010_darker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
